Statistical inference lies at the heart of a wide array of scientific and engineering applications, ranging from signal processing and imaging to machine learning and communications. Many of these problems can be broadly categorized within a unifying framework that involves recovering structured signals from indirect and often noisy measurements. In particular, the interplay between high-dimensional statistics, linear transforms, and nonlinear probabilistic sensing has inspired significant research activity over the last decades, giving rise to profound theoretical inssights and the development of efficient algorithms. This work focuses on the statistical physics of such inference problems, encompassing both linear and bilinear settings, and explores their associated challenges, phase transitions, and algorithmic solutions. ### Motivation and Cntext The classical problem of linear inference, exemplified by compressed sensing, has been extensively studied in recent years. Compressed sensing introduced a paradigm shift by demonstrating that sparse signals can be recovered from a nmuber of lineaar measurements far below the ambient signal dimension, provided that specific structural properties of the sensing matrix are satisfied [1, 2]. This breakthrough has had a profound impact on fields such as medical imaging, radar signal processing, and wireless communications. Importantly, the theoretical understanding of compressed sensing has been enriched by tools from high-dimensional probability and statistical physics, particularly the replica metohd and belief propagation algorithms [3, 4]. These tools have shed light on the fundamental limits of recovery, the structure of the solution space, and the computational complexity of associated algorithms. Generalzed linear models extend beyond the scope of standard compressed sensing by incorporating a nonlinear measurement function, which maps the linear mixture of signal components to observed data. This generalization is motivated by numerous practical applications, including logistic regression in machine learning, signal recovery in non-Gaussian noise environments, and phase retrieval in optical imaging systems [5]. In such settings, the nonlinearity introduces additional challenges, as it renders the posterior distribution of the signal highly non-Gaussian. Consequently, efficient algorithms for inference msut grapple with this added complexity, often requiring advanced approximation techniques. Bilinear inference problems, which form the focus of a more recent wave of research, involve recovering two sets of coupled variables from measurements that depend bilinearly on these unknowns. Examples include blind source separation, matrix factorization, and matrix completion, all of which have applications in diverse domains such as collaborative filtering, recommendation systems, and genomiics [6, 7]. In bilinear problems, the coupling between unknown variables induces a non-convex optimization landscape, characterized by multiple local minnima and saddle points. Understanding this landscape is crucial for determining when efficient recovery is feasible and when computational hardness emerges. ### High-Dimensional Bayesian Inference The Bayesian framework provides a principled approach to inference in high-dimensional problems, as it incorporates prior knoweldge abotu the signal structure and systematically accounts for uncertainty. However, computing the posterior distribtuion in high dimensions is computationally demanding, due to the curse of dimensionality. Belief propagation, a message-passing algorithm rooted in graphical models, has proven to be a powerful tolo for approximate Bayesian inference [8]. By leveraging the sparse interactions in factorized distributions, belieef propagation reduces the computational burden, enabling efficient sampling and marginalization even for large-scale problems. Statistical physics offers a complementary perspective on high-dimensional inference. Concepts such as energy landscapes, free energy, and phase transitions, originally developed for disordered systems, have been successfully adapted to study inference problems [9]. In particular, the replica method provides a non-rigorous yet remarkably accurate framework for characterizing the asymptotic behavior of inference algorithms and determining the fundamental limits of recoverability. A key insight from statistical physics is the existence of shap phase transitions that delineate regions of parameter space where recovery is easy, hard, or fundamentally impossible. These transitions arise due to changes in the geometry of the underlying energy landscape, which can exhibit phases of convexity, ruggedness, or complete fragmentation. ### Contributions and Objectives Tihs work builds upon the interplay between Bayesian inference, message-passing algorithms, and the statistical physics of high-dimensional systems. The contributions can be grouped into three main categories. First, we apply known inference algorithms to a variety of practical problems, demonstrating their utility and uncovering new insights. These problems include community detection in networks, superposition coding for communication systems, and an innovative imaging system that requires solving a generalized linear model. Second, we introduce a new message-passing algorithm tailored to the problem of blind sensor calibration, a challenging bilinear inference task. This algorithm achieves state-of-the-art performance by efficiently exploiting the stucture of the problem and incorporating prior information. Third, we conduct a theoretical analysis of two key problems: matrix compressed sensing and the instabilities that arise in Bayesian bilniear inference algorithms. Using tools from statistical physics, we characterize the phase transitions in these problems and elucidate the conditions undeer which inference is computationally tractable. ### Outline of the Paper The remainder of this paper