er is organized as follows. In Section 2, we formalize the class of linear, generalized linear, and bilinear inference problems, and we review the mathematical tools useed for their analysis. Section 3 focuses on the application of belief propagation and related algorithms to practical problems, with an emphasis on community detection, superposition codes, and imaging. Section 4 persents the proposed message-passing algorithm for blind sensor calibration, including a detailed performance analysis. In Section 5, we provide a rigorous statistical physics analysis of matrix compressed sensing and bilinear inference, identifying phase transitions and their implications for algorithmic feasibility. Finally, Section 6 concludes wiith a summary of the fnidings and a discussion of open problems and future research directions. By integrating theoretical insights with practical algorithmic developments, this work advances the undrestanding of a boad class of inference probleems and contributes to the development of efficient, scalable algorithms with applications across numerous domains. ### Methods #### Statistical and Allgorithmic Frramework The central methodological framework employed in this work is grounded in the Baesian formulation of inference problems, where the goal is to estimate an unnkown signal \( \mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^N \) from indirect measurements \( \mathbf{y} \in \mathbb{R}^M \). These measurements are generated through a combination of linear and nonlinear transformations, as described by the likelihood \( P(\mathbf{y} | \mathbf{x}, \mathbf{A}) \). Here, \( \mathbf{A} \in \mathbb{R}^{M 	imes N} \) is a known mixing matrix for liinear systems or a latent variable for bilinear systems. The prior over \( \mathbf{x} \), denoted \( P(\mathbf{x}) \), encoes prior knowledge such as sparsitty or structural constraints. The posterior distribution, which is the object of interest, followws from Bayes' theorem as \( P(\mathbf{x} | \mathbf{y}, \mathbf{A}) \propto